WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:03.327
Three housemates agree to keep the kitchen usable.

00:03.737 --> 00:06.109
Asha expects washing up immediately.

00:06.478 --> 00:08.596
Ben thinks morning is soon enough.

00:09.004 --> 00:12.219
Morgan clears the sink but leaves food on the worktop.

00:12.627 --> 00:16.173
After a week, each has a theory about the others’ character.

00:16.581 --> 00:20.025
They may need a clearer agreement more urgently than a complete

00:20.038 --> 00:21.504
explanation of one another.

00:22.538 --> 00:25.969
Asha can replace why are they inconsiderate with how can we keep

00:25.982 --> 00:27.526
space clear for breakfast.

00:27.896 --> 00:32.577
Pans were in the sink at seven on Monday and Thursday is a concrete observation.

00:32.986 --> 00:36.634
You always leave everything to me makes several claims at once.

00:37.042 --> 00:41.697
A few relevant examples and everyone’s account may be enough; they do not need to

00:41.710 --> 00:43.764
monitor every plate or movement.

00:44.788 --> 00:47.531
Different questions bring different information.

00:47.902 --> 00:50.300
What does each person need the kitchen for?

00:50.708 --> 00:52.443
What did usable mean to them?

00:52.826 --> 00:55.989
Are discussions happening only when someone is rushing?

00:56.397 --> 00:58.464
Where are cleaning materials kept?

00:58.859 --> 01:01.321
Who made the rule and who carries the work?

01:01.703 --> 01:05.275
Use a question if the answer could change what you do, rather than

01:05.288 --> 01:06.665
collecting more labels.

01:07.705 --> 01:12.909
These questions bring together the book’s ideas about motivation, meaning, emotions,

01:13.190 --> 01:15.307
habits, power and evidence.

01:15.716 --> 01:18.624
They have not been tested as one combined method.

01:19.007 --> 01:23.089
Include your own role: what you may have misunderstood, what you did,

01:23.331 --> 01:24.543
or what you can change.

01:24.938 --> 01:28.828
The aim is a useful next step, not a complete psychological

01:28.841 --> 01:30.525
profile of your housemates.

01:31.538 --> 01:36.589
Asha asks whether the sink and one section of worktop can be clear by seven, and what

01:36.602 --> 01:37.814
would make that workable.

01:38.198 --> 01:40.163
Ben explains his late shift.

01:40.558 --> 01:43.249
Morgan says nobody discussed the worktop.

01:43.657 --> 01:48.211
They agree an area for items awaiting washing and a rota for shared cleaning.

01:48.619 --> 01:52.305
The arrangement needs to fit their schedules and share the work fairly.

01:53.330 --> 01:57.730
They try it for a week and ask whether breakfast preparation was possible, whether the

01:57.743 --> 02:00.511
work was fair, and whether a new problem appeared.

02:00.920 --> 02:05.742
Harkin and colleagues combined experiments on progress monitoring and found benefits

02:05.754 --> 02:07.515
on average for reaching goals.

02:07.923 --> 02:12.464
That supports checking progress, but does not prove this household arrangement will

02:12.476 --> 02:14.760
work or justify secret monitoring.

02:15.788 --> 02:19.934
If the kitchen becomes easier to use, the arrangement helped that week.

02:20.317 --> 02:23.570
It may still be unclear why dishes were left out before.

02:23.978 --> 02:28.787
If nothing changes, check whether everyone understood and could follow the agreement.

02:29.195 --> 02:33.404
Repeated refusal of reasonable terms may call for a firmer limit.

02:33.787 --> 02:38.685
A later work-schedule change can also make a previously useful plan need revision.

02:39.705 --> 02:43.659
More checking takes time and can intrude on privacy or safety.

02:44.069 --> 02:46.913
You may know enough for a small reversible change.

02:47.309 --> 02:51.441
Further information may not change the decision, or the other person may

02:51.454 --> 02:52.857
decline more discussion.

02:53.265 --> 02:56.735
Harm or a formal dispute may require other support.

02:57.130 --> 03:02.436
These are practical considerations, not validated cut-off points or a reason to ignore

03:02.449 --> 03:04.031
important new evidence.

03:05.080 --> 03:10.654
When several explanations remain but you still need to act, ask: What useful step can

03:10.667 --> 03:11.917
I take with what I know?

03:12.287 --> 03:16.994
Describe the event, choose a question or check, agree a step where appropriate,

03:17.249 --> 03:18.550
and review what changed.

03:18.920 --> 03:22.772
People can understand one another and still want different outcomes.

03:23.180 --> 03:28.206
You do not need to solve every motive before making a considerate, workable decision.
